Based on my analysis of the diff and plan, I now have sufficient understanding to select appropriate specialist reviewer archetypes. The changes span:

1. **`scripts/design-log-publish.sh`** — new `design_artifact_excluded` deny arms for raw plan-review transcripts/sidecars
2. **`scripts/larch-log.sh`** — `round_artifact_included` refactor for dynamic Codex twins + retry exclusions
3. **`scripts/lib-design-round-artifacts.sh`** — `codex-plan-*` → `codex-primary-plan-*` rename in exclusions
4. **`scripts/test-check-reviewers.sh`** — new test helpers and assertions for stamp isolation, API-key leakage, and probe temp-home cleanup
5. **`scripts/test-design-log-publish.sh`** — extended deny-list test coverage for the new plan-review exclusions
6. **`scripts/test-design-structure.sh`** — new Check 21 (`assert_folded_sentinel_writes`) with fence extractor helpers
7. **`scripts/design-pause-load.md`** / **`docs/configuration-and-permissions.md`** / **`scripts/design-log-publish.md`** — documentation updates

The key risk surfaces are: glob pattern completeness and shadowing in the `case` blocks, correctness of the `round_artifact_included` allow/deny ordering, test assertions matching the real shell write literals, and API-key leakage surface in the probe temp-home harness.

{"archetypes":[{"name":"glob-coverage","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"Multi-arm case blocks in design_artifact_excluded and round_artifact_included are the primary deny/allow mechanism; gaps, overlaps, or ordering bugs silently leak or over-deny artifacts.","prompt_body":"Examine every `case` arm in `scripts/design-log-publish.sh design_artifact_excluded` and `scripts/larch-log.sh round_artifact_included`. For each arm, check whether the glob is anchored correctly (e.g. `codex-primary-plan-*-output*.txt` vs `codex-plan-*-output*.txt`) and whether any newly added deny arm is shadowed by — or fails to shadow — a prior allow arm. Verify that the arm ordering produces the documented behavior: dynamic Codex retry deny before the broad allow, explicit dynamic Codex allow before the catch-all `*-output*.txt` allow, and the plan-review transcript denies before any structural sidecar suffix denies that would otherwise match phased suffixes. Flag any filename that a new allow arm can accidentally admit from a context the plan intends to exclude, and any filename from the test fixtures in `scripts/test-design-log-publish.sh` that would not be caught by the current deny arms. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-assertion-fidelity","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"assert_fence_write_before_pause and assert_step_sentinel_inside_guard match literal shell write strings; a wrong sentinel token produces a false pass.","prompt_body":"Inspect the new helpers `extract_bash_fence_after_marker`, `extract_bash_fence_containing`, `assert_fence_write_before_pause`, and `assert_step_sentinel_inside_guard` in `scripts/test-design-structure.sh`. For each helper, verify that the literal needle strings (e.g. `current-design-env-$PPID.sh`, `design-pause-save.sh`, `: > \"$DESIGN_TMPDIR/.completed/step-X\"`) will match exactly what `skills/design/SKILL.md` Bash fences are expected to contain, given whitespace-tolerant fence extraction (`^[[:space:]]*\\`\\`\\`bash`). Check whether `extract_bash_fence_after_marker` correctly handles a fence whose opening/closing triple-backtick line has leading whitespace but its body lines do not, and whether `extract_bash_fence_containing` terminates on the first matching fence or may silently skip it. Verify the `after_pause` branch of `assert_fence_write_before_pause` checks the correct ordering (`source < pause < sentinel`) and that the `step-6` cleanup-fence call passes `true` correctly. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"secret-leakage","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"New Codex probe tests specifically assert that OPENAI_API_KEY sentinels do not leak into TMPDIR; verifying those assertions are structurally sufficient and correct is security-critical.","prompt_body":"In `scripts/test-check-reviewers.sh`, examine every new test fixture that uses `OPENAI_API_KEY=<REDACTED-TOKEN>` or `<REDACTED-TOKEN>`. For each fixture, verify that the leakage check (`grep -Fr '<REDACTED-TOKEN>' \"$SCRATCH/t...\"`) covers all subdirectories written during the probe, including any temp CODEX_HOME created by the production code under test. Check whether `assert_no_probe_homes` is called at the right point (after the probe completes but before the SCRATCH dir is cleaned up) and whether its `find` command targets the correct directory. Verify that the legacy-strip fixture correctly checks that the original fixture file is unchanged (`cmp -s`) and that the captured temp config does not retain `api_key`, `model_provider`, or `env_key` larch-specific fields. Identify any fixture where a partial HOME isolation or a shared SCRATCH subdirectory could cause a false pass on the leakage check. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"lib-round-artifact-sync","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"lib-design-round-artifacts.sh, its .md doc, and the edit-in-sync rule require both to change together; the rename from codex-plan to codex-primary-plan must be consistent across all consumer call sites.","prompt_body":"Verify that the rename from `codex-plan-*-output.txt` to `codex-primary-plan-*-output.txt` in `scripts/lib-design-round-artifacts.sh design_round_artifact_included` is reflected consistently in `scripts/lib-design-round-artifacts.md`, `scripts/design-log-publish.md`, and `scripts/design-log-publish.sh`. Check that no remaining reference to the old glob `codex-plan-*-output.txt` (without the `-primary-` infix) appears in any of these files in a context where it would admit or deny files it should not. Also confirm that the edit-in-sync rule in `lib-design-round-artifacts.md` lists `scripts/test-lib-design-round-artifacts.sh` and that the test file was updated in the same diff to cover the new glob pattern; if the test file is absent from the diff, flag the missing synchronization. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
